## Error analysis
{% set w = model.weakest_segment %}{% set ep = model.error_profiles %}

```python
errors = pd.read_csv(run / "segment_errors.csv")
display(errors[["attribute", "group", "n", "small_sample", "average_precision", "roc_auc", "ece", "calibration_slope"]].round(3))
display(pd.read_csv(run / "error_profiles.csv").drop(columns="key").round(3))
display(pd.read_csv(run / "model_profit_check.csv").round(2))
```

**Interpretation.** Ranking inside a segment is weaker than overall (lowest: {{ w.attribute }} = {{ w.group }}, ROC-AUC {{ w.roc_auc | num(3) }}), because contract, tenure and internet type are the strongest signals. Calibration is weakest for senior customers, the group the targeting contacts most often. Flagged customers who stayed resemble those who left ({{ ep.fp.month_to_month_share | pct(0) }} vs {{ ep.tp.month_to_month_share | pct(0) }} month-to-month); missed churners average {{ ep.fn.mean_tenure | num(0) }} months of tenure. In money, the three candidates are within {{ model.profit_check_spread_validation | usd }} of each other on validation, so the simpler model is a safe choice.

**So what.** The model is good at ranking and its probabilities are trustworthy enough for expected-value arithmetic. It does not tell us which customers an offer would keep; that remains an assumption tested by the pilot.
